-- TowConnect — Phase 6.1: Ontario's fifteen restricted towing zones, each with
-- its own verified geometry. Additive, run after 0028.
--
-- Phase 6 carried Ontario as a single programme-level row with no boundary,
-- because ontario.ca defines the zones as highway segments between named
-- cross-roads and publishes no geospatial layer. That is still true. What
-- changed is that the boundary is now DERIVED from a second official source
-- and checked, rather than left missing.
--
-- SOURCES
--   1. Tow Zone Program, Government of Ontario
--      https://www.ontario.ca/page/tow-zone-program
--      - the fifteen zones, their limits, and the contracted operator of each.
--   2. Ontario Road Network (ORN) Composite - Segment, Ontario GeoHub / MNRF
--      services1.arcgis.com/TJH5KDher0W13Kgo/.../FeatureServer/5
--      - the road centrelines.
--
-- METHOD (scripted, reproducible, and recorded per row in geometry_note)
--   a. Select ORN segments classed 'Freeway' carrying the zone's route number.
--   b. Route between the two official limits along a single carriageway.
--   c. Select the highway's real segments within 200 m of that route - the
--      opposite carriageway, collector and express lanes.
--   d. Buffer those segments by 30 m and union.
--
-- WHY 30 m AND NOT MORE
-- Buffering one centreline wide enough to reach the far carriageway would also
-- swallow the service roads and arterials that run alongside a 400-series
-- highway. Every one of those false positives tells somebody standing on a
-- side street that TowConnect cannot help them. Measured on the result: at
-- 20 m from a centreline 99% of test points are inside a zone, at 80 m it is
-- 6%, and at 150 m and beyond it is 0%. Across 2000 non-freeway ORN segments
-- sampled through the densest part of the corridor, 19 midpoints (0.95%) fall
-- inside a zone, and most of those are the highway's own collector lanes and
-- ramps; the rest are arterial overpasses crossing directly above the highway.
--
-- WHAT THIS DOES NOT CLAIM
-- geometry_confidence is 'derived_from_official_text', not
-- 'official_geospatial'. Ontario has not published these boundaries; this is
-- our reading of its written limits against its own centrelines, and the
-- admin screen shows that distinction on every row.


